<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:06c79cedbb63cf3e468fda4d2194c99da4c320ea>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """El presente conjunto de datos contiene a los quetognatos de las Colecciones Biológicas  adscritas al Centro de Colecciones Científicas (CCC) de la Universidad del Magdalena. El recurso cuenta con 3 registros marinos que fueron recolectados en distintos localidades oceánicas profundas del Caribe colombiano. Los lotes fueron obtenidos a partir de muestras de macrofauna como parte de proyectos de evaluaciones biológicas en diferentes Bloques de exploración de hidrocarburos, desarrollados por entidades externas que posteriormente depositaron los ejemplares en la colección. Los especímenes están depositados bajo el acrónimo CBUMAG: CHA.
La colección fue documentada, digitalizada y publicada por primera vez durante la ejecución del proyecto BID-CA2020-055-NAC "Incremento de la accesibilidad de información marina de las Colecciones de Historia Natural de Colombia, mediante la movilización de datos, el fortalecimiento de la infraestructura y el entrenamiento". El presente conjunto de datos es el producto de dicho proceso realizado entre 2021-07-01 y 2023-06-30, como parte del entregable “Colección de biología marina del Centro de Colecciones Biológicas de la Universidad del Magdalena – CBUMAG” aportado por la UniMagdalena al proyecto.
This data set contains the chaetognaths of the Biological Collections attached to the Center for Scientific Collections (CCC) of the Universidad del Magdalena. The resource has 3 marine records that were collected in different deep ocean locations in the Colombian Caribbean. The lots were obtained from macrofauna samples as part of biological evaluation projects in different hydrocarbon exploration Blocks, developed by external entities that subsequently deposited the specimens in the collection. The specimens are deposited under the acronym CBUMAG: CHA.
The collection was documented, digitized and published for the first time during the execution of the BID-CA2020-055-NAC project "Increasing the accessibility of marine information of the Natural History Collections of Colombia, through data mobilization, infrastructure strengthening and training". The present dataset is the product of that process carried out between 2021-07-01 and 2023-06-30, as part of the deliverable "Marine Biology Collection of the Biological Collections Center of the University of Magdalena - CBUMAG" contributed by UniMagdalena to the project.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Chaetognatha, Quetognatos, Caribe colombiano, Marino, Colección biológica, Universidad del Magdalena, CBUMAG, Specimen" ;
    ecsummary:name "Quetognatos de las Colecciones Biológicas de la Universidad del Magdalena" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/219460e6-ceb3-42eb-8216-fa1d2d09a3c1> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:06c9867d5cc5c1c8f894f237200b8bc16d0977fc>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Marine Conservation Research

Dataset credits:
Oliver Boisseau, Marine Conservation Research International

Abstract:
The dataset contains all sightings of cetaceans and other megafuana made during the MAPS spring cruise in Apr/May 2019 (NMFS permit 14809). When conductiong visual effort, two observers were situated either side of R/V Song of the Whale on an observation platform with an approximate eye-height of 5.5 m above sea-level during daylight hours. The observers scanned the trackline and in front of the vessel using naked eye; 7 x 50 binoculars were used to identify species and estimate group size. During periods of bad weather, observers were stationed on deck with an approximate eye-height of 3.5 m above sea-level.

Purpose:
The MAPS (Marine Mammal Passive Acoustics and Spatial Ecology) cruises form part of a BOEM project to describe the distribution and behaviour of deep-diving cetaceans in the offshore waters of North Carolina (NMFS permit 14809). MAPS is a cooperative agreement between the Bureau of Ocean Energy Management and the University of North Carolina Wilmington in partnership with Duke University.

Supplemental information:
Environmental attributes were recorded less frequently than the GPS data and not all GPS records get environmental attributes attached.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Marine Animal Survey,Marine Biology,Marine mammals,Sea turtles,Visual Sighting,Vessel, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Sightings from R/V Song of the Whale during the spring 2019 MAPS survey (NMFS permit 14809)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/90399c94-5dc8-4281-be23-c6b492782b56> ;
.

