<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:0856cedc5df31f62938ca39cfe1599791cf5177e>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """El presente conjunto de datos contiene a los moluscos de las Colecciones Biológicas adscritas al Centro de Colecciones Científicas (CCC) de la Universidad del Magdalena. Este recurso cuenta con 266 registros de moluscos marinos, todos provenientes de diferentes sitios del Caribe colombiano esto incluye la zona costera y  de mar abierto. Los especímenes están depositados bajo el acrónimo CBUMAG: MOL.
La colección fue documentada, digitalizada y publicada por primera vez durante la ejecución del proyecto BID-CA2020-055-NAC "Incremento de la accesibilidad de información marina de las Colecciones de Historia Natural de Colombia, mediante la movilización de datos, el fortalecimiento de la infraestructura y el entrenamiento". El presente conjunto de datos es el producto de dicho proceso realizado entre 2021-07-01 y 2023-06-30, como parte del entregable “Colección de biología marina del Centro de Colecciones Biológicas de la Universidad del Magdalena – CBUMAG” aportado por la UniMagdalena al proyecto.
This data set contains the molluscs from the Biological Collections attached to the Center for Scientific Collections (CCC) of the Universidad del Magdalena. This resource has 266 records of marine mollusks, all from different sites in the Colombian Caribbean, this includes the coastal zone and the open sea. The specimens are deposited under the acronym CBUMAG: MOL.
The collection was documented, digitized and published for the first time during the execution of the BID-CA2020-055-NAC project "Increasing the accessibility of marine information of the Natural History Collections of Colombia, through data mobilization, infrastructure strengthening and training". The present dataset is the product of that process carried out between 2021-07-01 and 2023-06-30, as part of the deliverable "Marine Biology Collection of the Biological Collections Center of the University of Magdalena - CBUMAG" contributed by UniMagdalena to the project.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Espécimen, CBUMAG, Mollusca, Caribe colombiano, marino" ;
    ecsummary:name "Moluscos de las Colecciones Biológicas de la Universidad del Magdalena" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/58e533c3-8f4b-4fc6-ae44-20f85ecf58c8> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:08581727e1d29bff153e3ca68806cbcf970db398>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """These data sets include the picoplankton component analysed by flow cytometry from samples collected as part of the Integrated Marine Observing Systems (IMOS) National Mooring Network -National Reference Station (NRS) field sampling.


Historical data collected as part of CSIRO Coastal Monitoring program is available for three of the National Reference Station sites:

Maria Island Coastal Station Data 1944-2008, Port Hacking 100m Coastal Station Data 1953-2010 and Rottnest Island Coastal Station Data 1951-2009.
Nine Sites are included in the IMOS NRS field sampling stations. Maria Island (TAS), Kangaroo Island (SA), Esperance (WA), Rottnest Island (WA), Ningaloo (WA), Darwin (NT), Yongala (QLD), North Stradbroke Island (QLD) and Port Hacking 100 (NSW).
Sampling at these sites is conducted by several members of IMOS NRS Biogeochemical project. Sampling at the sites began between 29/9/2008 and 8/11/2010 and is currently ongoing for 7 sites. Sampling ceased at Ningaloo and Esperance (WA) sites in Aug/Sept 2013.
Sampling is conducted monthly (Esperance and Ningaloo were 3 monthly) off small vessels. Depths sampled range from surface to 105 metres depending on the site. Depths are usually sampled at 10 metre intervals. Details including information on station locations, depths sampled and sample types collected are shown in the PDF link below extracted from the IMOS NRS NATIONAL REFERENCE STATIONS BIOGEOCHEMICAL OPERATIONS - A PRACTICAL HANDBOOK, Version 2.2 July 2012. A link to the full handbook is also provided which includes full details of the sampling regime and analyses.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen" ;
    ecsummary:name "IMOS National Reference Station (NRS) - Picoplankton flow cytometry, Australia (2008 - 2018)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/d18d8386-a08a-4459-98f1-c95220847bed> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:085901723c30b9b9511276ff70711db2dc98034f>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Pendoley Environmental

Dataset credits:
Data provider
West Australian Sea Turtle Satellite Tracking Project
Originating data center
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/tracking/">Satellite Tracking and Analysis Tool (STAT)</a>
Project partner
BHP Billiton Quantum ProjectCare for Hedland AssociationPendoley Environmental Pty Ltd
Project sponsor or sponsor description
BHP Billiton Quantum Project


Abstract:
Flatback turtles are being satellite tracked from Cemetery Beach in the town of Port Hedland in the Pilbara region of Western Australia. This is the second year of this project and with 5 transmitters deployed in 2008/2009 and an addtional 10 transmitters deployed during the 2009/2010 season. The project is being sponsored by BHPBIO as part of the Quantum Project.

Supplemental information:
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/tracking/index.shtml?project_id=357">Visit STAT's project page</a> for additional information.

This dataset is a summarized representation of the telemetry locations aggregated per species per 1-degree cell.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Cemetery Beach Port Hedland Flatback Tracking Project  2009/2010 (aggregated per 1-degree cell)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/215c984f-b790-49bb-ac65-6eaadcfee536> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:086b3b060c197a224369e77c4f236500d4f3994e>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "Considered the \"king\" of sea lions, the Steller sea lion (Eumetopias jubatus) is the largest of all sea lions and enjoys a lifespan of up to thirty years. In Canada, the Steller can be spotted along the rocky coast of British Columbia. The hefty marine mammals typically travel alone or in small groups, but they congregate in large numbers at traditional rookeries and haul-outs during the mating and pupping season. The population was severely depleted in Canada but following its protection in 1970, the size of the adult population has more than doubled." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Samplingevent, Steller sea lion, British Columbia" ;
    ecsummary:name "Steller sea lion (Eumetopias jubatus) counts and haulout locations British Columbia" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/4da863f1-ab83-4af1-afbe-7a61f2e81054> ;
.

