<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:13be6579d11bbff8a52da52630c8f8ab61966d69>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Encana Corporation

Dataset credits:
Marielle Thillet, Deep Panuke Project, Encana Corporation

Abstract:
We present marine mammal observation statistics, high-frequency seismic source characteristics, and example denoising of marine mammal acoustical recordings using data collected during the mitigation and monitoring program for a 3-D seismic survey by Encana Corporation, Calgary, Alberta, Canada, in the northwest Atlantic during 2003. Marine mammals were observed both visually and acoustically. No marine mammal incidents or adverse reactions were observed during the survey.

Acoustical observations were made by the Seamap Passive
Acoustic Cetacean Monitoring System (SPACMS), consisting of
two hydrophones placed 50 m apart, towed ahead of and to one
side of the seismic source. Visual and acoustical detections were uncorrelated, indicating the complementary nature of the two observational techniques. Visual detections were more common per hour of effort than acoustical detections. Acoustical detection rates showed no significant day–night difference.

Marine mammals appeared to have avoided very close ranges (100 m) from the seismic array during seismic acquisition, but the overall number of marine mammals in the observable radius (1–2 km) did not change significantly when the seismic source was “on” compared to “off.” Marine mammals were observed in larger groups and appeared to have become less vocal when the seismic source was active. It should be noted however, that the results from this data gathering effort may be affected by potential sources of bias (such as the combination of data from toothed and baleen whales).

Signal processing of seismic source signatures indicated some
high-frequency energy content consistent with expectations from
earlier work. This analysis confirmed that most of the seismic
energy was concentrated at lower frequencies (500 Hz). No
low-frequency comparisons with near-field data could be made
due to the geometry of the SPACMS recording hydrophones
and seismic source, which resulted in the Lloyd’s mirror effect
obliterating low-frequency components in the SPACMS records.
A wavelet-based denoising method was applied to improve the visibility of marine mammal vocalizations on a spectrogram display.

Supplemental information:
Visual data collected in this survey are registered on OBIS-SEAMAP separately as the <a href="http://seamap.env.duke.edu/datasets/detail/650">Deep Panuke whale sightings 2003</a> dataset.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Deep Panuke whale Acoustic 2003" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/b2412f4d-5988-4876-bc7c-954cbe192cc8> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:13c1c4f18377b06fa7255398912e7c7e1ca731aa>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "This dataset describes the abyssal megafaunal mollusc morphotypes observed during the two ABYSSLINE cruises (AB01/MV1313 - 2013 and AB02/TN319 - 2015) to the UKSRL polymetallic-nodule exploration contract area (UK-1) and the site EPIRB (~250 km east of the UK-1 area) in the Clarion-Clipperton Zone in the Pacific Ocean. These megafaunal molluscs were recorded using ROV and AUV. There appear to be at least 4 distinct morphospecies identified by imagery. This dataset is from the paper: Amon D, Ziegler A, Drazen J, Grischenko A, Leitner A, Lindsay D, Voight J, Wicksten M, Young C, Smith C (2017) Megafauna of the UKSRL exploration contract area and eastern Clarion-Clipperton Zone in the Pacific Ocean: Annelida, Arthropoda, Bryozoa, Chordata, Ctenophora, Mollusca. Biodiversity Data Journal 5: e14598. https://doi.org/10.3897/BDJ.5.e14598" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence; Deep-sea mining; Polymetallic nodule; Clarion-Clipperton Zone; megafauna; Mollusca" ;
    ecsummary:name "abyssline_uksrl_contract_area_ccz_megafauna_2013-2015_mollusca" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/a7a1ee38-425e-44be-af06-480e33889d03> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:13cc470083a1e67ec81ebb3422160a2338ed31d0>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "El Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia que hace parte del INVEMAR, cuenta con una colección de Tejidos creada en el año 2011 a partir de 931 lotes de muestras de moluscos, crustáceos y corales, los cuales fueron obtenidos en el marco de diferentes proyectos realizados por el Programa Biodiversidad y Ecosistemas Marinos del instituto en colaboración con entidades externas como Ecopetrol y las Universidades del Valle y de los Andes. Actualmente la colección conserva una parte de los lotes bajo condiciones de criopreservación (-20°C y -80°C), y otra parte de lotes se mantiene preservados en alcohol (96%) en el área de Colecciones Húmedas del Museo bajo ambiente controlado a 20°C. El presente conjunto de datos incluye 7 registros biológicos de tejidos de esponjas obtenidos en el Caribe colombiano, que han sido catalogados e identificados a especie. " ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen, Porifera; Esponjas; Tejidos; Demospongiae; Caribe colombiano; Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia; MHNMC" ;
    ecsummary:name "Colección de Tejidos del Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia – Makuriwa (Invemar)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/1d94f371-a3e5-4e9a-b48c-f26e864c7621> ;
.

