<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:27fd67645fa196fadf286644000b52feb7611df0>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """This dataset contains occurrence records associated with a study describing the seasonal changes in biomass and species composition of the major groups of macrozooplankton of Bedford Basin, an area polluted with domestic sewage. Sagitta elegans Verrill was the dominant species in both numbers and biomass of predatory macrozooplankton.

Abundance information (numbers per cubic metre) and biomass (grams per cubic metre) are provided.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "BioChem: Macrozooplankton biomass measurements in Bedford Basin, 1969-1971" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/f9822123-91ba-4f3b-a87d-369a7d073b05> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:27ff4f1796b4ac1e0957295a08746eff0f1ff547>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """This dataset is a subset of the crustaceans collection of the Muséum national d'Histoire naturelle (MNHN - Paris) extracted from GBIF and filtered for the southwest Pacific region and for marine species only.
The full collection covers all groups of crustaceans (except the very rare Remipedia and Tantulocarida). Consisting of some 100 000 inventoried lots arranged in systematic order, and with about 6000 type specimens, this collection is one of the richest in the world for its diversity. The collection was established before 1800 with the collections of the explorer naturalists (voyages of Bougainville, Savigny, Dumont D'Urville ...) and then continued, notably with those of the Travailleur and the Talisman. The collection has benefited from the work of prestigious scientists like Jean-Baptiste Lamarck, Latreille, H. and A. Milne-Edwards, and Chevreux. The specimens, measuring 1 mm to over 2 m in size, are mainly preserved in alcohol. Some are kept dry (old collections such as the historical collection of crabs), others as slide preparations. The collection includes worldwide material of marine, freshwater and terrestrial crustaceans (including pest species). The collection grows 1 to 1.5% per year on average, varying according to the group, thanks to numerous donations and deposits. For 25 years the "Tropical Deep-Sea Benthos" (formely MUSORSTOM) expeditions have resulted in the largest collection in the world from the deep-water (bathyal) environment.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen" ;
    ecsummary:name "The crustaceans collection (IU) of the Muséum national d'Histoire naturelle (MNHN - Paris) in the southwest Pacific region" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/0c9c73d0-7fa1-4c6d-b18b-9affde19eb60> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:27ff72e6c4fc7d6c30735df35e9c922ee5da064e>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "This is a historical dataset, published in 1940, regarding the marine algae of Alexandria. The survey was conducted by A. H. Nasr, Assistant Director of the Marine Biological Station in Ghardaqa, Egypt and it was facilitated by the Fuad I Institute of Hydrobiology and Fisheries in Alexandria, Egypt. The dataset contains occurrence data of the algae found in the wider marine region of Alexandria within the year 1933." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Metadata, Mediterranean, Egypt, Algae, Samplingevent" ;
    ecsummary:name "The marine algae of Alexandria. I. A report on some marine algae collected from the vicinity of Alexandria by A. H. Nasr (1940). Notes and Memoirs No. 36" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/53fff777-5dd5-4e5b-8638-aa0396e0513b> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:2806c777ed1a200504a89a60912aa30765d69e72>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Happywhale

Dataset credits:
Happywhale and contributors

Abstract:
Happywhale.com is a resource to help you know whales as individuals, and to benefit conservation science with rich data about individual whales.

Supplemental information:
Sightings and images were submitted to Happywhale by contributors. A portion of the Happywhale data were transferred to OBIS-SEAMAP upon the agreement between Happywhale and OBIS-SEAMAP.

There may be duplicate records among Happywhale datasets and other OBIS-SEAMAP datasets. The precision of date/time vary per record. Some records have date accuracy up to year only.

This dataset includes sightings and photos from the following 7550 contributors in alphabetic order:

