<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:2dce323e49b87d7bbd22689fa8f0bb289781808b>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
ProDelphinus

Dataset credits:
Data provider
ProDelphinus tracking project
Originating data center
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/tracking/">Satellite Tracking and Analysis Tool (STAT)</a>


Abstract:
ProDelphinus is a Peruvian NGO whose aim is the conservation of threatened and endangered marine fauna which have interaction with Peruvian fisheries. We also work hard to promote environmental education and build capacity in local fishing communities with the goal of promoting the long&#8208;term sustainability of both the fisheries and the species with which they interact.

Supplemental information:
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/tracking/index.shtml?project_id=1203">Visit STAT's project page</a> for additional information.

This dataset is a summarized representation of the telemetry locations aggregated per species per 1-degree cell.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "ProDelphinus Peru Leatherback Tracking Project (aggregated per 1-degree cell)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/e3c359b0-ac7f-4360-a788-6e19e286d155> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:2dd096735ad453447f86ccc986a96521017e2bbe>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """El Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia que hace parte del Instituto de Investigaciones Marinas y Costeras Invemar, custodia entre su material biológico la Colección de Brachiopoda. Esta fue creada en el año 2009 a partir de ejemplares obtenidos principalmente durante la expedición Corales de profundidad-Marcoral realizada en el año 2005 y unos pocos lotes de las expediciones previas de Macrofauna I (1997) y II (2001), ambas llevadas a cabo por parte de este Instituto en el Caribe colombiano. A partir de la revisión taxonómica de ese material Rojas-Briceño  et al. (2009) realizaron unos primeros registros para el país y derivado de su revisión, se organizó el material disponible alcanzando unos 53 lotes con los cuales se inicia la colección. En el año 2017, posterior al traslado del Museo a la nueva sede del Invemar, se logró consolidar la colección con la asignación de un espacio específico para el grupo. Actualmente, la colección catalogada cuenta con 96 lotes del Caribe colombiano, obtenidos entre los 0.2 y los 3194 m de profundidad sobre fondos blandos de la plataforma y el margen continental, mayoritariamente en inmediaciones del área de Corales del Rosario y San Bernardo. Los registros biológicos correspondientes a este material catalogado se encuentra incluidos en el presente recurso, el cual fue documentado a partir del proceso de curaduría y catalogación efectuado en 2023 durante el desarrollo del proyecto BID-CA2020-055-NAC.
The Marine Natural History Museum of Colombia, which is part of the Marine and Coastal Research Institute Invemar, holds among its biological material the Brachiopoda Collection. This was created in 2009 from specimens obtained mainly during the Deep Corals - Marcoral expedition in 2005 and a few lots from the previous expeditions of Macrofauna I (1997) and Macrofauna II (2001), both carried out by this Institute in the Colombian Caribbean. Based on the taxonomic review of this material, Rojas-Briceño et al. (2009) made the first records for the country and, as a result of their review, the available material was organised, reaching some 53 lots with which the collection was started. In 2017, after the Museum moved to the new Invemar headquarters, the collection was consolidated with the allocation of a specific space for the group. Currently, the catalogued collection has 96 lots from the Colombian Caribbean, obtained between 0.2 and 3194 m depth on soft bottoms of the shelf and the continental margin, mostly in the vicinity of the area of Corales del Rosario and San Bernardo. The biological records corresponding to this catalogued material are included in the present resource, which was documented from the curation and cataloguing process carried out in 2023 during the development of the BID-CA2020-055-NAC project for the strengthening of the collections.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen, Brachiopoda, Colecciones biológicas, Marino, Caribe Colombiano, MHNMC, Biological Collections of Natural History, Marine, Colombian Caribbean, SiB_MARINO" ;
    ecsummary:name "Colección Brachiopoda del Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia – Makuriwa (Invemar)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/9812f083-84a3-4709-9e59-610ff7247fe6> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:2dd6b4d7f93ffff02885210c5503b38042c03b52>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
National Parks and Wildlife Service, Dept. of Environment, Heritage and Local Government

Dataset credits:
National Parks and Wildlife Service, Dept. of Environment, Heritage and Local Government
Michelle Cronin, Coastal &amp; Marine Resources Centre, University College Cork

Abstract:
The status of Ireland's harbor seal <i>Phoca vitulina vitulina</i> population is poorly understood. The most recent national population estimate dates back to the breeding season in 1978 and did not cover the entire coastline. Reliable up-to-date information on the abundance and distribution of harbor seals in Ireland is necessary to assess the conservation status of the species and for the effective identification, management and monitoring of special areas of conservation required for harbor seals under the European Union Habitats Directive. To provide comprehensive current information on Ireland's harbor seal population, a geographically extensive survey was conducted along the coastline of the Republic of Ireland during the species' annual moult in August 2003. This complemented a similar survey of Northern Ireland, which was conducted in 2002. Using thermal imaging technology, haul-out groups of harbor seals and grey seals <i>Halichoerus grypus</i> were identified from the air, aerial-counts were obtained and compared with simultaneous ground-count data from selected sites. Harbor seal distribution recorded during the 2003 moult season was concentrated in the south-west, west and north-west of the country. This national survey yielded a minimum population estimate for the Republic of Ireland of 2905 harbor seals, delivering an effective baseline for current and future population research.

Supplemental information:
This dataset also includes ancillary grey seal data, showing summer terrestrial distribution of grey seals. This data cannot be used for estimating population size of grey seals as this is conducted during the winter breeding season.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,harbour seal,minimum population estimate,census,thermal imagery,Aircraft,Sightings, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Harbor seals in Republic of Ireland in Aug 2003" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/55fdf06d-205c-4de0-80d5-4e9c355b54ee> ;
.

