<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:5678869adf8990791adc5bf7f6a2708042587d34>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "Occurrence (presence/absence) of the crabs at Kanamai Beach, Bamburi, Mkomani and the Gazi mangrove forest, and salinity measurements at various seepage points in Gazi, Nyali, Bamburi, Kanamai, Mkomani, Shelly and Tiwi measured during the months of June, July and August 1985. The data were digitized by VLIZ from the original report: Polk, P. (Ed.) (1986). Kenya report. VUB: Brussel. 358 pp." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Mangrove forests, Seepage, Species diversity, Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Faunal species composition of brackish water crabs at Gazi, Kanamai, Bamburi and Mkomani (Kenya)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/96ffebcf-1d28-4ba0-bd4d-258f1bfe8d31> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:567ab6dc935c88163ae1d26122a2aa47587cce27>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "Understanding the drivers of species' geographic distribution has fundamental implications for the management of biodiversity. For coral reef fishes, mangroves have long been recognized as important nursery habitats sustaining biodiversity in the Western Atlantic but there is still debate about their role in the Indo-Pacific. Here, we combined LA-ICP-MS otolith microchemistry, underwater visual censuses (UVC) and mangrove cartography to estimate the importance of mangroves for the Indo-Pacific coral reef fish Lutjanus fulviflamma in the archipelago of New Caledonia. Otolith elemental compositions allowed high discrimination of mangroves and reefs with 83.8% and 98.7% correct classification, respectively. Reefs were characterized by higher concentrations of Rb and Sr and mangroves by higher concentrations of Ba, Cr, Mn and Sn. All adult L. fulviflamma collected on reefs presented a mangrove signature during their juvenile stage with 85% inhabiting mangrove for their entire juvenile life (about 1 year). The analysis of 2942 UVC revealed that the species was absent from isolated islands of the New Caledonian archipelago where mangroves were absent. Furthermore, strong positive correlations existed between the abundance of L. fulviflamma and the area of mangrove (r = 0.84 for occurrence, 0.93 for density and 0.89 for biomass). These results indicate that mangrove forest is an obligatory juvenile habitat for L. fulviflamma in New Caledonia and emphasize the potential importance of mangroves for Indo-Pacific coral reef fishes." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Extent of Mangrove Nursery Habitats Determines the Geographic Distribution of a Coral Reef Fish in a South-Pacific Archipelago" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/329b1c55-6957-45b4-a272-08af7639930f> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:567c6722fe903732767084304c02bf86f305f407>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Happywhale

Dataset credits:
Happywhale and contributors

Abstract:
Happywhale.com is a resource to help you know whales as individuals, and to benefit conservation science with rich data about individual whales.

Supplemental information:
Sightings and images were submitted to Happywhale by contributors. A portion of the Happywhale data were transferred to OBIS-SEAMAP upon the agreement between Happywhale and OBIS-SEAMAP.

There may be duplicate records among Happywhale datasets and other OBIS-SEAMAP datasets. The precision of date/time vary per record. Some records have date accuracy up to year only.

This dataset includes sightings and photos from the following 28 contributors in alphabetic order:

Allie Smith; Charlotte Kirchner; Christian Wegener; Corinne Eichler; Cottele Benny; Dennis Maloney; Era Horton; Eric Angel Ramos; Espaço Talassa; Holly Morgan-Wynne; Iuliia; Janiek Schrijer; Kimberly Nesbitt; Lara Eade; Marilia Olio; Matthew Gledhill; MS Spitsbergen; OSI Cetis; Paula Hoppe; Per Nikolaj Bukh; Phillippa Tichotova; Rob Rector; Ryan Donnelly; Sabine+Ulf; Sara Franson; steve; Whale Wonders BVI; Zeal Goolesby""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,PhotoID;Visual sighting, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Happywhale - Common bottlenose dolphin in North Atlantic Ocean" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/0ac3ee87-ce2a-47b6-bd08-2909f54705e5> ;
.

