<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:58c4cd75a50bcecbd72547a2eae2eff802ec00c5>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "La infraclase Euthyneura (Heterobranchia: Mollusca) incluye especies que exhiben gran diversidad en formas, tamaños y coloraciones. En Colombia, es posible encontrar especies de este clado tanto en el Caribe como en el Pacífico, asociadas a diversos ecosistemas (e.g., arrecifales coralinos y rocosos). A pesar de la importancia que reviste el conocimiento de los invertebrados marinos asociados a un ecosistema, y en este caso particular a los moluscos de la infraclase Euthyneura, los estudios sobre este tipo de organismos son básicamente inexistentes en la región del Caribe insular colombiano. Por el contrario, estudios sobre los moluscos con concha en sitios cercanos al continente son más fáciles de encontrar. Con el objetivo de determinar la biodiversidad de Heterobranchia: Euthyneura, presente en los ecosistemas arrecifales submareales someros de la Isla Cayos de Bolívar, se realizaron muestreos usando la metodología ECORAPs a través de careteo y buceo libre en los rangos de profundidad de 0,20 a 35 m. Se encontraron 14 especies de babosas y liebres marinas que fueron fotografiadas e identificadas in situ y de las cuales se recolectaron algunos especímenes que fueron preservados en viales con etanol al 96%. Lo anterior, permitió obtener una lista de especies que permite ampliar los reportes previos y aportar al conocimiento y conservación del grupo, y generar un catálogo visual de las especies de Euthyneura asociadas a los ecosistemas arrecifales de la Isla Cayos de Bolívar en la Reserva de la Biosfera Seaflower." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Moluscos, Diversidad, Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Heterobranquios observados en la Expedición Seaflower Isla Cayo Bolivar 2022 - Proyecto Colombia BIO" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/7c38fae2-1505-44eb-abfa-44e633dfbdc8> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:58c9fbe1b97be03953e76141243dae8ebc2ab55b>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Annette Broderick

Dataset credits:
Data provider
Marine Turtle Research Group
Originating data center
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/tracking/">Satellite Tracking and Analysis Tool (STAT)</a>
Project partner
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/mtrg/"> Marine Turtle Research Group</a> (Annette Broderick, Wayne, Fuller and Brendan Godley)of the University of Exeter, UK, and the Society for the Protection of Turtles in Northern Cyprus (SPoT)
Project sponsor or sponsor description
Our satellite tracking projects have been supported by <a href="http://www.apachecorp.com/about/"> Apache Corporation</a>,<a href="http://www.bp.com/"> BP Egypt</a>, <a href="http://www.britishchelonia.org/">British Chelonia Group</a>, <a href="http://www.kthy.net/"> Cyprus Turkish Airlines</a> and <a href="http://www.nerc.ac.uk//">Natural Environment Research Council</a>. In addition, Liff Primary School have supported the MTCP over many years with their fund-raising efforts. We are now looking for other commercial sponsors and schools/youth groups who might like to join us in the tracking of more turtles in 2005. You can contact us on MTRG@seaturtle.org


Abstract:
The <a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/mtrg/projects/cyprus/"> Marine Turtle Conservation Project</a> is a project of the <a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/mtrg/"> Marine Turtle Research Group</a> and has been monitoring the nesting beaches of Northern Cyprus since 1992. The project is in collaboration with the Society for the Protection of Turtles in Northern Cyprus (SPoT) and the Department of Environmental Protection. In Northern Cyprus both green and loggerhead turtles nest. Green turtles in the Mediterranean are listed as critically endangered by the <a href="http://iucn.org//">IUCN</a>, with estimates suggesting that there are as few as 300-400 females nesting annually in this region. Loggerhead turtles nest in slightly greater numbers, with approximately 2000-3000 females nesting annually. Approximately 30 % of the green and 10% of the loggerhead turtles in the Mediterranean nest in Northern Cyprus.

Supplemental information:
<a href="http://www.seaturtle.org/tracking/index.shtml?project_id=7">Visit STAT's project page</a> for additional information.

This dataset is a summarized representation of the telemetry locations aggregated per species per 1-degree cell.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Northern Cyprus 2004: Loggerhead &amp; Green Turtles (aggregated per 1-degree cell)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/7da2613f-909e-47d8-9563-94e817825e2a> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:58d23b67d52e4132bfb5a0f0e659991ca09a2e88>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """The Museum of Tropical Queensland, Townsville, is home to our internationally recognised collection of hard corals. The collection is a comprehensive coverage of Indo-Pacific Scleractinia, particularly from the Great Barrier Reef of Australia.

The collection also includes:

- the J.E.N. Veron collection, donated by the Australian Institute of Marine Science;

- comparative material from Indonesia, Maldives, Seychelles, Papua New Guinea and many other tropical nations.
Data from ALA (http://collections.ala.org.au/public/show/co200). Taxa matched to World Register of Marine Species.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen" ;
    ecsummary:name "Queensland Museum Corals" ;
