<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:6435edc4f481b98970121921049d9614163b788b>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
APEM Limited

Dataset credits:
APEM Inc. and Normandeau Associates Inc.

Abstract:
Equinor Wind US, LLC were committed to undertaking a Construction and Operation Plan (COP) of their lease area located in the New York Bight (OCS-A 0520). To facilitate this, surveys were undertaken by APEM Inc. and Normandeau Associates, Inc. (Normandeau-APEM Team) to provide baseline information for consideration in the assessment of potential impacts.

Purpose:
Equinor initiated monthly offshore high resolution aerial surveys of marine mega-fauna in the New York Bight offshore wind lease area plus a 4 km (2.5 mile) buffer. The surveys used APEM Inc.'s ultra-high resolution aerial digital still imagery to characterize birds present in the lease area and gather additional information on other marine fauna. These surveys provided the baseline information required for conducting impact assessments. The dataset is comprised of observations and survey track line information collected from one survey per month from November 2017 until October 2018 inclusive (12 surveys in total). 

APEM Inc. and Normandeau Associates, Inc. (Normandeau–APEM team) extracted and identified avian and other marine species and provided quality control (QC) of the results.

Supplemental information:
[2021-08-23] No-animal objects (e.g. boats) were given species names and codes. These wrong assignments are corrected.

Each record represents 1 individual (except for Fish Shoals which is one entry per 1 shoal encompassing uncounted number of fish). 

Locations of digital snapshots are available as a static point layer.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Marine Animal Survey,Marine Biology,Seabirds,Visual Sighting,Aircraft, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Empire Wind Digital Aerial Wildlife Surveys for BOEM Lease Area OCS-A 0520, Equinor Wind US LLC, November 2017-October 2018" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/cd28a3e1-f146-4638-85ae-a5ea6b739144> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:6437609a87af62014b5c90566d05deaeada44253>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "Penguins were tracked from a breeding colony on Montague Island (Baranguba) (36°15.11 S, 150° 13.60 E), 9 km off the southeast coast of New South Wales, Australia. Tracking was conducted during September and October over a 5-year period from 2015 to 2019. Penguins were tracked while brooding young chicks (0–4 weeks old) and therefore limited to making single-day foraging trips within approximately 25 km of the colony. This dataset contains additional tracking in the period 2012 to 2014. Data sourced from Dryad repositories referenced in the cited publications." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Tracking of Little Penguins (Eudyptula minor) from Montague Islands, New South Wales coast, Australia (2012-2019)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/33edf19e-249d-4d20-993b-7c7f34e7bace> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:6441216bf15e214856d3c6ea64ec8ce7278ec886>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """In 2008 as part of university masters thesis project two studies were completed, one experimental and one mensurative, on the abundance of primary producers and consumers from rocky intertidal habitats in Atlantic Canada.

As part of the mensurative study, four wave-sheltered locations were sampled spanning 350 km of Nova Scotia coastline. Data from that study describe the abundance of seaweeds and invertebrates found at three tidal elevation zones (high, middle, and low) for a total of 1170 quadrats. Measurements include the abundance (percent cover) of the different taxa found at each quadrat using a 20 cm × 20 cm frame.

For data associated with the manipulative part of the study see Watt and Scrosati 2014.

Because of its taxonomic amplitude and coverage of a wide environmental stress gradient, this data set is potentially useful to address in novel or infrequent ways other broad ecological issues, such as abundance–occupancy relationships, species co-occurrence, species abundance distributions, dominance and rarity, spatial scales of population and community variability, and distribution of phylogenetic diversity.

This dataset includes both presence and absence occurrence records.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Abundance of intertidal algae and invertebrates on the Atlantic coast of Nova Scotia" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/51122f08-680a-48a4-9c4f-77033e5e9532> ;
.

