<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:6f1f4102ce047e0692968f1767ad40218f62f094>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Caroline Weir

Dataset credits:
Caroline Weir, Ketos Ecology
Karen Stockin, Sea Watch Foundation

Abstract:
Spatial and temporal trends in the distribution of the harbor porpoise <i>Phocoena phocoena</i>, white-beaked dolphin <i>Lagenorhynchus albirostris</i>, and minke whale <i>Balaenoptera acutorostrata</i> were assessed at a coastal North Sea study area in Aberdeenshire, Scotland. Between March 1999 and October 2001, cetacean data were collected using both land- and vessel-based survey methods. A total of 174 sightings of these three cetacean species was recorded during approximately 330 h of combined survey effort (228 h land-based and 102 h vessel-based).

Purpose:
The data were collected to analyze the distribution and seasonality of cetacean species in the region, in order to provide recommendations for their long-term conservation and management.

Supplemental information:
This dataset is accompanied with a <a href="http://seamap.env.duke.edu/datasets/detail/423">land-based survey dataset</a>, a separate dataset available on OBIS-SEAMAP.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Harbour porpoise,white-beaked dolphin,minke whale,North-east Scotland,distribution,seasonality, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Harbour porpoises, white-beaked dolphins and minke whales in North Sea - Vessel surveys -" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/344cf0a8-e322-44e8-8d57-0f6ec795b6ca> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:6f1f43f38650ed1c845bf53a506ce13ccf3fe0d6>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "The Eviota zebrina complex includes eight species of closely-related dwarf gobies, four of which are herein described as new. The complex is named for Eviota zebrina Lachner &amp; Karnella, 1978, an Indian Ocean species with the holotype from the Seychelles Islands and also known from the Maldives, which was once thought to range into the Gulf of Aqaba and the Red Sea eastward to the Great Barrier Reef of Australia. Our analysis supports the recognition of four genetically distinct, geographically non-overlapping, species within what was previously called E. zebrina, with E. zebrina being restricted to the Indian Ocean, E. marerubrum sp. nov. described from the Red Sea, E. longirostris sp. nov. described from western New Guinea, and E. pseudozebrina sp. nov. described from Fiji. The caudal fin of all four of these species is crossed by oblique black bars in preservative, but these black bars are absent from the four other species included in the complex. Two of the other species within the complex, E. tetha and E. gunawanae are morphologically similar to each other in having the AITO cephalic-sensory pore positioned far forward and opening anteriorly. Eviota tetha is known from lagoonal environments in Cenderawasih Bay and Raja Ampat, West Papua, and E. gunawanae is known only from deeper reefs (35–60 m) from Fakfak Regency, West Papua. The final two species are E. cometa which is known from Fiji and Tonga and possesses red bars crossing the caudal fin (but lost in preservative) and a 9/8 dorsal/anal-fin formula, and E.oculineata sp. nov., which is described as new from New Guinea and the Solomon Islands, and possesses an 8/7 dorsal/anal-fin formula and lacks red caudal bars. Eviota oculineata has been confused with E. cometa in the past." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen" ;
    ecsummary:name "Data from: A review of the Eviota zebrina complex, with descriptions of four new species (Teleostei, Gobiidae)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/10a356d8-7021-42bd-b4dc-8f9d6db32f0b> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:6f2156d2983cc451945e6558a13e344b17c932cc>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Happywhale

Dataset credits:
Happywhale and contributors

Abstract:
Happywhale.com is a resource to help you know whales as individuals, and to benefit conservation science with rich data about individual whales.

Supplemental information:
Sightings and images were submitted to Happywhale by contributors. A portion of the Happywhale data were transferred to OBIS-SEAMAP upon the agreement between Happywhale and OBIS-SEAMAP.

There may be duplicate records among Happywhale datasets and other OBIS-SEAMAP datasets. The precision of date/time vary per record. Some records have date accuracy up to year only.

This dataset includes sightings and photos from the following 11 contributors in alphabetic order:

Christian Harvey; Craig Emery; David  Bailey; Doug Fishman; Era Horton; Erin Theis; James Moskito; Joshua Meza-Fidalgo; Marilia Olio; Mary Stanford; Paul Pudwell""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,PhotoID;Visual sighting, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Happywhale - California sea lion in North Pacific Ocean" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/b06015c1-d2fe-4a20-8325-1a500857bec7> ;
.

