<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:79809eed126da70f90dfe67de974f735f44a1330>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """El Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia - MHNMC, que hace parte del Instituto de Investigaciones Marinas y Costeras Invemar, custodia la colección de moluscos marinos mas grande del país, constituida por un estimado de aproximadamente 95000 especímenes, que corresponden a 11337 lotes catalogados. La colección fue creada desde los inicios del Invemar en 1963, y está conformada mayoritariamente por los ejemplares obtenidos durante las actividades de investigación de la institución, así como también provenientes de depósitos realizados por investigadores nacionales e internacionales, y de otras entidades del país. Cuenta con especímenes recolectados desde 1955 hasta el 2023, tanto del Caribe (mayoritariamente) como del Pacífico colombiano, y también de algunos países del Caribe Sur. La colección custodia material tipo de gastrópodos y bivalvos, principalmente de paratipos, y de holotipos en un menor número.  La presente versión de este recurso incluye los registros biológicos correspondientes a 3500 lotes obtenidos entre los 0 y 520 m de profundidad, los cuales fueron verificados y documentados a partir del proceso de curaduría y catalogación realizado entre 2022 y 2023 durante el desarrollo del proyecto BID-CA2020-055-NAC.
The Museum of Marine Natural History of Colombia - MHNMC, which is part of the Marine and Coastal Research Institute Invemar, holds the largest collection of marine molluscs in the country, consisting of an estimated 95000 specimens, corresponding to 11337 catalogued lots. The collection was created at the beginning of Invemar in 1963 and is mainly made up of specimens obtained during the institution's research activities, as well as from deposits made by national and international researchers, and from other entities in the country. It has specimens collected from 1955 to 2023, both from the Caribbean (mostly) and the Colombian Pacific, and also from some countries of the Southern Caribbean. The collection holds type material of gastropods and bivalves, mainly paratypes, and a smaller number of holotypes.  The present version of this resource includes the biological records corresponding to 3500 lots obtained between 0 and 520 m depth, which were verified and documented from the curatorial and cataloguing process carried out between 2022 and 2023 during the development of the project BID-CA2020-055-NAC.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen, Mollusca, Moluscos, Colecciones biológicas, Caribe Colombiano, Pacífico colombiano, MHNMC, Mollusk, Biological Collections, Colombian Caribbean, Pacific Colombian" ;
    ecsummary:name "Colección de Moluscos del Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia - Makuriwa" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/a121d740-8e25-40f0-a06c-3e5d0fbac6c3> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:798949c17517f0cfa41df0e02e0df77a06e42fa7>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """This dataset comprises the phytoplankton abundance and biovolume estimates of a broader sampling program carried out by the Plankton National Reference Station within the Integrated Marine Observing system. Regular monthly phytoplankton samples are undertaken at the stations as part of a larger water sampling program by the Australian National Reference Stations. In total nine stations are sampled regularly around the Australian coastline. Sampling is conducted monthly (Esperance and Ningaloo 3 monthly) off small vessels at each of the nine reference stations. The sampling is an integrated water sample collected with niskin bottles at 10m depth intervals and preserved in lugols solution. The depth of the sample varies at each station. The plankton ecology lab (based in Queensland) will analyse the samples collected for: 1. Community composition / abundance (cells/l) 2. Biovolume (um3/L). Data storage and access is planned to be interoperable with other national and international programs through the IMOS Infrastructure. Station metadata can be found through this resource. Data is available freely via the AODN portal: https://portal.aodn.org.au. As the taxonomic resolution of the data has changed over time, due to continual training, it is important that users refer to the change log tables included in your data download. These will provide information on the validity of the taxa, from what date we have been identifying certain taxa etc. Classification fields may be blank depending on the level to which that taxa has been identified, i.e. if only identified to family, genus and species will be blank.
Data downloaded from IMOS 14-March-2017 from https://catalogue-imos.aodn.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/metadata.show?uuid=c13451a9-7cfc-091c-e044-00144f7bc0f4 and taxonomy matched to WoRMS. Added additional records on 2022-03-01""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "IMOS National Reference Stations (NRS) Phytoplankton Abundance and Biovolume" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/ab737464-f691-4b72-aae2-1bb70411468d> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:798fdfffb5e2508994db5ab5a57b8682719f623d>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
University of Rhode Island

Dataset credits:
University of Rhode Island
Bureau of Land Management
US Dept of the Interior

Abstract:
The Cetacean and Turtle Assessment Program (CETAP) was sponsored by the Bureau of Land Management and conducted between October 1978 and January 1982 by the University of Rhode Island in outer continental shelf waters of the northeastern United States from Cape Hatteras, North Carolina, to the Gulf of Maine and from shore to 9.3 km (5 nautical miles) seaward of the 1829 m (1000 fathoms) isobath. Population surveys were conducted by CETAP to assess the distribution and abundance of whales, dolphins and sea turtles inhabiting the study area.

Purpose:
This dataset is a subset of CETAP sightings, extracting opportunistic sighting records, with the addition of taxonomic information of species sighted. The full dataset may be available upon request. Requests for more data, further details on the dataset, proper use of the dataset, and questions should be directed to the primary contact person.

Supplemental information:
[2020-09-30] The following invalid species names were corrected according to the Integrated Taxonomic Information System (ITIS).
Turtles: Testudines (173749) =&gt; Testudines (948936)

Sighting points that are more than 2 km inland were eliminated. Sightings located from 1 km to 2 km inland can be considered as on-water sightings. Those points have TRUE in [Excluded] field to indicate that they seem to fall on land but may be correct sightings.

Metadata are in progress.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Marine Animal Survey,Marine Biology,Marine mammals,Sea turtles,Aircraft,Visual Sighting, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "BLM CETAP SHIP Sightings" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/d11b19e7-4103-4cf5-ad4e-8c6c2cfb2e6d> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:7994ea6b6d20d78295696653e7272f06d596cb28>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "Lancaster Sound mooring data has been used to monitor the oceanographic fluxes existing in the Arctic Ocean and to determine ocean and ice statistics for marine shipping and offshore development. As in other Arctic regions, this is the only means to monitor oceanographic and pack ice seasonal and inter-annual variability and supported annually by Canadian Coast Guard icebreakers. Specific unique instrumentation were designed and built to obtain this sparse data set and represent an required data input to ecosystem-based management, climate studies and navigation safety. Important data source for Ecosystem based Management, for Climate studies and for navigation safety. Plankton samples were collected as part of this program to characterize the water masses flowing from the Arctic Ocean to the Atlantic Ocean." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "oceans, biota, biological classification, Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "BioChem: Canadian Arctic ThruFlow Study plankton collection" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/7fa785bd-da15-43f3-8b74-2ccc76396381> ;
.

