<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:94accfceb597e967d494598f601ee5167c963d80>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Between August 7 and September 23, 1975, data on marine seston and phytoplankton were obtained from one litre water samples collected by a pump collecting water samples from Hudson Bay, Canada. 

Survey results consist of density (number of individuals per litre). 
The original survey report also contains information on methods and results of salinity, chlorophyll a concentration, ATP concentration, POC and PON measurements. The OBIS view of this dataset includes only the species abundance information.

This research was collected with the Canadian Hydrographic Services and involved continuous sampling along pre-determined transects through Hudson Bay. This presented a unique opportunity to collect samples on a spatial and temporal frequency never before possible during a single sampling season from the entire area of Hudson Bay.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "oceans, biota, biological classification, Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "BioChem: Seston and phytoplankton data from Hudson Bay, 1975" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/d64c0db0-78b6-4596-890a-97144cfe549e> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:94b0efec53646de612a5faff810cd322f96b85dc>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """The TT 01/2008 survey was a follow up survey to the SS 01/2008 to sample beds of high quality live, sub-fossil and fossil corals off SE Tasmania, and to document and sample the communities in depths up to 4000 m using the deep-diving Remotely operated vehicle (ROV) Jason. 16 dives were completed in or close to areas in the South-east Commonwealth Marine Reserves; the ROV Jason spent almost 400 hours underwater, took over 36000 photographs and 300 hours of video of the deep-reef, and collected over 1000 biological specimens, most new to science.
The focus of the survey was collection of fossil corals (Desmophyllum sp.), description of habitats at depth between 700 and 4000 m depth and establishing two long-term monitoring sites in the Huon CMR (settlement plates). It explored and sampled on the near vertical slice in the earth's crust, known as the Tasman Fracture Zone, which drops from approximately 2000 metres to over 4000 metres. Jason was used to collect video, high definition still images (mosaiced images) and selective samples of fossil corals and invertebrate fauna.
Reports, metadata and other data from this voyage are at https://www.marine.csiro.au/data/trawler/survey_details,cfm?survey=TT200801
""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Metadata, Specimen, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "CSIRO Survey TT200801 - ROV Jason cruise tn228: South and East of Tasmania" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/a003e72f-d8e5-4d94-9aed-4bc50862c4ad> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:94b47d223d04a7b88cf82147d499a01cd76162cd>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "Caspian Sea is the biggest land-locked saltwater lake in the world with a catchment area of 3.5 million km2. It is located between 36° N and 62°N. The basin of Caspian Sea is divided into three distinct physical regions: Northern, Middle, and Southern Caspian. Phytoplanktons are the autotrophic components of the plankton community and a key part of marine and freshwater ecosystems. They obtain energy through photosynthesis, as do plants on land. This means phytoplankton must receive light from the sun, so they live in the surface layers of oceans and lakes. The collected data of the phytoplanktons of the Caspian Sea are related to a part of the studies conducted from 1996 to 2021. These data are obtained from various sources such as (articles, theses, research projects, etc.)." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Phytoplankton; Caspian Sea; Iran, Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Phytoplankton of the Iranian part of Caspian Sea- part1" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/c7407a9c-8adb-4022-91d5-e4abe2ae1be0> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:94bde51cf21f636fdd7071857dd0a12e3ce27679>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """The PELD (Long-term Program of Ecological Research) Coastal Habitats of Espírito Santo is part of the Brazilian LTER program and started in 2017. It is located on the Eastern Coast of Brazil, and includes coastal ecosystems including estuaries, mangroves, coastal reefs and rodolith beds. This is an innovative project to assess the temporal patterns in multiple coastal ecosystems in the Eastern Brazil Marine Ecoregion, where there is a decadal warming trend. The program associates research and conservation, at three Conservation units: Costa das Algas Environmental Protected Area (APACA), Santa Cruz Wildlife Refuge (RVSSC) and Municipal Reserves of Integral Protection of the mangroves of Piraquê-Açu and Piraquê-Mirim rivers (RPPAM).

The project aims to investigate the interactions among abiotic, climatic and ecological dynamics in the benthic communities and Ichthyofauna assemblies associated with the estuaries, the mangroves and the Rhodolith beds.

PELD-HCES counts with a research team coordinated by UFES in partnership with USP, UFBA, UFSB, UFF, UFPR, UNISUL, UFSC and the State University of Oregon, and funded by CNPQ, CAPES and FAPES.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Samplingevent, ILTER, Coastal Habitats of Espírito Santo, Costa das Algas, rocky reef, intertidal zone, coastal biodiversity, recruitment" ;
    ecsummary:name "Brazil Reef Biodiversity - ILTER Coastal Habitats of Espírito Santo (Benthic Invertebrate Recruitment)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/da5c1e1c-d36c-4f19-a446-ebede37828a4> ;
.

