<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:98b1361d22d843d4d6d8df174d56f61aa4df036e>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """This survey is a first attempt at determining the distribution and relative abundance of all seabird species occurring in Macquarie Island waters, especially in relation to the main bathymetric and oceanographic features of the study area, namely the Macquarie Ridge and Subantarctic Front, respectively. To this end, observations of seabirds were made from aboard the CSIRO Research Vessel Southern Surveyor while it conducted acoustic, trawling and benthic surveys in the waters immediately round Macquarie Island and northwards along the Macquarie Ridge during 14-31 January 1999.
The nine main sites within the study area and the distribution of seabird counts at each site are shown in Figure 1 (see Appendix 4 of https://www.frdc.com.au/project?id=1942). Most observations were made over the relatively shallow waters of the main part of the Macquarie Ridge i.e. close inshore off the east and west coasts of the island and northward along the ridge to the southern edge of the Subantartic Front Gap (sites 2-5). Additional observations were made in shallow waters well south of the island at the southernmost part of the ridge (site 7), and at two other ridge sites north of the Subantarctic Front Gap (sites 8 and 9). Observations were also carried out at two deep-water (pelagic) sites: at site 6 in waters c.25 miles west of Aurora Trough, and at the Subantarctic Front Gap (site 1) immediately north of the main north-south ridge through the island.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Observations of seabirds in Macquarie Island waters (1999)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/2befbc59-5758-4406-8b63-3d6e738fa990> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:98b5e2f2bb091602e14ebf197a32c5cf88b2f3a8>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """In 1982 the Arctic Laboratories were commissioned to undertake a combined chemical, biological and physical oceanographic survey from March 1981 to April 1982 in the vicinity of the Issungnak O-61 artificial island. Because of its position near the 20m depth contour, the island provided an ideal opportunity to study oceanographic features in a transitional zone between the more truly marine waters of the outer shelf and Mackenzie River dominated water of shallower nearshore regions.  

Zooplankton collection
Sampling included deployment of vertical ring nets (mesh 158 microns). This resource presents the zooplankton distribution information recorded in the study's report (Erikson et al, 1983). Occurrence records include abundance information recorded as numbers per cubic metre. 

Ice flora and Phytoplankton collection
Ice fauna intimately associated with the ice-water interface were sampled with an ice-scraping device made from a plastic funnel (25cm diameter opening) and plankton net attached to a hinged aluminum rod. A sample consisted of revolutions of the scraper around the perimeter of the 30 cm ice auger hole.
All samples were preserved with acidic Lugol's solution. determination of ice flora and phytoplankton species composition and numbers were made using a Zeiss phase contrast inverted microscope at magnifications up to 625x following the Utermohl technique. Permanens slides were examined and cells identified as completely as possible. See page 11 of interim report for more information. These occurrence records are not available in this version.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "BioChem:  Zooplankton collected at Issungnak Artificial Island in Southern Beaufort Sea, 1981-1982" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/fb346773-5a98-4e9a-8712-24a3a730e0ee> ;
.

