<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a1f60f84406f1ebb920799a3649dd4d4f23d431b>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Observatoire pour la Conservation et l'Etude des Animaux et Milieux Marins (OCEAMM)

Dataset credits:
Observatoire pour la Conservation et l'Etude des Animaux et Milieux Marins (OCEAMM)

Abstract:
Small cetaceans can use a variety of habitats and are generally wide-ranging. For this reason, extensive sighting surveys to assess their distribution, abundance, and habitat preference are needed. In this study, we conducted systematic surveys to document the distribution of the harbor porpoise observed in the English Channel (North Sea, France). The English Channel is an epicontinental sea. It constitutes a relatively narrow link between the Atlantic Ocean and the North Sea and is influenced by strong tides and freshwater inputs, which create well-mixed waters. The harbor porpoise is placed under Appendix II of the European Union Habitats Directive, and an assessment of his status and distribution in the English Channel, very exposed to human activities, is much needed to identify conservation and management strategies.
Therefore, we conducted surveys following a series of predetermined saw-tooth transect lines to study the distribution of the harbor porpoise (<i>Phocoena phocoena</i>).  
Boat transect were conducted using a Sealine Stateman 35, equipped with two 230 hp Volvo inboard engine at speed around 10 knots. Surveys were made as many days as possible throughout the season at sea state of Beaufort 3 or less, between March 2009 and September 2011.
For each animal sighting, the species, angle of sighting, the distance from the boat, the group size and composition, the general behavior and the swimming direction were recorded.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Vessels,Sightings,Harbour porpoise,Phocoena phocoena,English Channel, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "OCEAMM harbor porpoise sightings in the North Sea" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/e9d28b60-2fe8-48a4-89ec-a1f3ad46607c> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a1f86eacd43550ffdb2799b910d6cf7956cb00d7>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "In this dataset, tissue samples from gill and pereopods were collected from ethanol preserved anomuran specimens deposited in the referral center of the Center for Marine Living Resources and Ecology, India. The specimens were collected onboard FORV Sagar Sampada between 2016 and 2020 from shallow as well as deep-sea. A 573–629 bp fragment of cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COI) and 354–516 bp fragment of 16S rRNA was amplified and sequenced. The sequence generated is being used to support the respective species' morphological taxonomic status. It is also being used to study the phylogenetic relationship with their congeners." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Anomura, Crustacea, DNA barcoding, COI, 16s., 16S rRNA, Cytochrome oxidase (COI)" ;
    ecsummary:name "DNA barcoding of the Anomuran crabs of Indian EEZ using COI and 16S rRNA genes" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/d7bf65ae-d272-4ee1-a296-d4f33bca827e> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a1fea52fe259a5814080ac416b374f5a5d8d3017>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Adult P. urinatrix were studied at two colonies within the Auckland region of New Zealand, namely Kauwahaia Island (36° 53′ 13″ S, 174° 26′ 12″ E) on Auckland’s west coast and in the Tasman Sea, and Burgess (Pokohinau) Island of the Mokohinau Island group (35° 54′ 10″ S, 175° 07′ 05″ E) within the Hauraki Gulf (Figure 1). Breeding adults were captured by hand from marked study burrows during late incubation (Burgess Island, n = 7) or chick rearing
(Kauwahaia, n = 5) and tracked using geolocation-immersion loggers from the British Antarctic Survey.
Data and metadata from Zoatrack (https://zoatrack.org) projects 108 and 111 accessed on 2021-06-01""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Common Diving petrel  (Pelecanoides urinatrix) tracking from Kauwahaia Island and Burgess Island near Auckland, New Zealand (2010-2012)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/1709e714-6737-4a78-a812-696671d4f734> ;
.

