<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a572c1885ddf7f5d9df71894d9f0354f9e0e3dd4>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
NOAA Southwest Fisheries Science Center (SWFSC)

Dataset credits:
Marine Mammal and Turtle Division, Southwest Fisheries Science Center, NMFS, NOAA

Abstract:
This 2000 eastern tropical Pacific cetacean line-transect survey was part of the Stenella Abundance Research Project (STAR), a multi-year cetacean and ecosystem assessment study designed to assess the status of dolphin stocks which have been taken as incidental catch by the yellowfin tuna purse-seine fishery in the Eastern Tropical Pacific. This survey was conducted by the NOAA Southwest Fisheries Science Center aboard NOAA ships <i>David Starr Jordan</i> and <i>McArthur</i> for a combined total of approximately 31,000 kilometers surveyed from late July to early December 2000. This dataset provides the time-date and geographical coordinates, by species/stock, of all cetaceans detected during the survey, as well as the daily and intra-daily start and end points of the line-transect survey by the NOAA Ship  <i>David Starr Jordan</i>.

Purpose:
The primary purpose of the survey was to collect information necessary to estimate abundance of dolphin stocks in the eastern tropical Pacific that are taken as bycatch in the yellowfin tuna purse seine fishery.

Supplemental information:
This dataset is supplemental to the following datasets: 
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1986, Cruise 989
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1986, Cruise 990
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1987, Cruise 1080
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1987, Cruise 1081
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1988, Cruise 1164
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1988, Cruise 1165
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1989, Cruise 1267
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1989, Cruise 1268
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1990, Cruise 1369
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, MOPS 1990, Cruise 1370
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 1998, Cruise 1610
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 1998, Cruise 1611
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 1998, Cruise 1612
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 1999, Cruise 1613
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 1999, Cruise 1614
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 2000, Cruise 1616
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 2003, Cruise 1623
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 2003, Cruise 1624
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 2006, Cruise 1630
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 2006, Cruise 1631
SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR-LITE 2007, Cruise 1634""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Vessels,Sightings,Cetaceans,Whales,Dolphins,Stenella,Delphinus,Population,Abundance,Survey,Census, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "SWFSC Marine Mammal Survey, STAR 2000, Cruise 1615" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/4a694757-eec3-446a-b730-c3bea4c479e3> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a57791dd3f754e0d620be32c4962a49e83cc9245>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """El Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia - MHNMC que hace parte del Instituto de Investigaciones Marinas y Costeras Invemar, custodia entre su material biológico la Colección de Nemertinos (Gusanos cinta). Esta fue creada en el año 2008 a partir de tres lotes que contenían 33 especímenes; aunque se tenía conocimiento de la existencia previa de más lotes, los cuales estuvieron durante varios años confundidos con material de otros grupos de vermes. En el año 2016 durante el proceso de organización del Museo, tras el traslado del Invemar a nuevas instalaciones, el material extraviado fue ubicado; se destinó un espacio físico para esta colección y a partir de allí se dio paso al proceso paulatino de catalogación.  Actualmente la colección custodia 323 lotes catalogados, recolectados en diferentes ambientes desde el litoral en la plataforma, hasta el margen continental profundo entre los 0 y 3896 m de profundidad. El material proviene principalmente del Caribe, aunque también se cuenta con algunos especímenes del Pacífico colombiano. Este recurso presenta los registros biológicos correspondientes a todo el material catalogado que fue documentado a partir del proceso de curaduría y catalogación efectuado entre 2022 y 2023 durante el desarrollo del proyecto BID-CA2020-055-NAC.
The Marine Natural History Museum of Colombia - MHNMC, which is part of the Marine and Coastal Research Institute Invemar, holds among its biological material the Collection of Nemertines (Tapeworms). This was created in 2008 from three vauchers containing 33 specimens; although there was knowledge of the previous existence of more lotes, which were for several years confused with material from other groups of worms. In 2016, during the process of organizing the Museum, following the transfer of Invemar to new facilities, the missing material was located; physical space was set aside for this collection, and from there the gradual process of cataloging began.  The collection currently holds 323 cataloged lots, collected in different environments from the littoral on the shelf to the deep continental margin between 0 and 3896 m depth. The material comes mainly from the Caribbean, although there are also some specimens from the Colombian Pacific. This resource presents the biological records corresponding to all the cataloged material that was documented from the curatorial and cataloging process carried out between 2022 and 2023 during the development of the project.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Specimen, Nemertea, Nemertinos, Gusanos cinta, Colecciones biológicas, Marino, Caribe Colombiano, Pacífico colombiano, MHNMC, Nemerteans, Ribbon worms, Biological colections, Marine, Colombian Caribbean, Pacific Colombian" ;
    ecsummary:name "Colección de Nemertinos del Museo de Historia Natural Marina de Colombia - Makuriwa" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/26ff98fc-cfe1-444e-82ad-6e087b7d86fe> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a5782cc6af58892260d05b100a1bc35c6ccb8117>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description "This dataset pertains to the outputs of the Belgica 121 (B121) expedition, which aim was to explore the surroundings of the Gerlache Strait (Western Antarctic Peninsula) and to carry out a detailed biodiversity census focusing on intertidal and shallow areas using both classic descriptive marine ecology methods as well as state-of- the art techniques (habitat mapping, genetics, trophic ecology). This dataset will offer access to the raw data on biodiversity occurrences, obtained using a range of methods." ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Samplingevent, benthos, intertidal, shallow, marine biodiversity, Antarctic Peninsula, Southern Ocean, low-environmental impact, meiobenthos" ;
    ecsummary:name "The Belgica 121 expedition to the Western Antarctic Peninsula: a high resolution biodiversity census" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/7eb2ccad-2063-4ea8-b4e3-164dbc852fce> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a588afe04cdb0c7cdd7f6dab9b0dc0339d86bf48>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """The Australian Aerial Waterbird Surveys (AWS) database stores temporal and spatial waterbird data on individual species, their breeding status and estimates of wetland area, collected during annual aerial surveys, extending back to 1983. The core methodology for these surveys has remained the same. The database includes three principal survey programs: The Eastern Australian Waterbird Survey, the National Waterbird Survey and surveys of the major wetland sites in the Murray-Darling Basin. Since 1983, the Eastern Australian Waterbird Survey has covered about a third of the continent each October, representing one of the larger and longer running biodiversity surveys in Australia, sampling wetland and waterbird communities across 2.7 million km2 of eastern Australia. In 2008, we did aerial surveys over most large wetlands across Australia during a period of two months. Since 2010, we have comprehensively surveyed all the major wetlands in the Murray-Darling Basin.
This dateset is subset of species that have been identified as marine in the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS). The data can be downloaded at https://aws.ecosystem.unsw.edu.au/""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Australian Waterbird Surveys (1983-2018)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/0a92deef-a73d-469b-b3fd-5d3e5f2ba2a0> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:a58d546a6430c881f94377f6fa44a41044c3cbf7>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Tracking study to identify the migration strategies of two very closely related shorebird species, the Curlew sandpiper (Calidris ferruginea) and the Red-necked stint (Calidris ruficollis), migrating from the same non-breeding site in Australia to similar breeding sites in the high Russian Arctic.
Based on 13 Curlew sandpiper and 16 Red-necked stint tracks from light-level geolocator tags, individual Curlew sandpipers make use of fewer stopover areas along the flyway compared to Red-necked stints. Furthermore, and notably during northward migration, Curlew sandpipers have a higher dependency on fewer sites, both in terms of the percentage of individuals visiting key stopover sites and the relative time spent at those sites. While Curlew sandpipers rely mainly on the Yellow Sea region, that has recently experienced a sharp decline in suitable habitat, Red-necked stints make use of additional sites and spread their relative time en-route across sites more evenly.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Tracking Red-necked stints (Calidris ruficollis) and Curlew sandpipers (Calidris ferruginea)  along the  East Asian-Australasian Flyway (EAAF)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/f47b3e69-0124-4fb8-b6fd-a655dd2c935a> ;
.

