<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:b915502f323eb6aff9c490c6b192cce77cdfff4c>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """BIOGEOMAR | Dataset | Mollusca | Gastropoda 

The BioGeoMar Program was created in 2019 with the support of the Boticário Group Foundation for Nature Protection in order to analyze the state of knowledge of marine biodiversity in Brazil, describe how biodiversity is distributed along the coast, and point out priority areas for conservation and carry out future studies. The program gathered 18 researchers, 9 postgrads, and 6 research assistants, from different expertise representing 12 public universities in Brazil, working in a collaborative network. Global platforms are being used that obtain information on the geographic distribution of the species and unprecedented data for science from the zoological and botanical collections of the researchers' laboratories are being accessed. All the data will be publicly available in national and international digital repositories. BioGeoMar is also promoting continuous training and contributing to the UN Ocean Decade. University Consortium: Universidade Federal do Rio de Janeiro, Universidade Federal de Minas Gerais, Universidade Federal do Paraná, Museu Nacional/UFRJ, Universidade Estadual de Londrina, Universidade Federal de Alagoas, Universidade Federal Fluminense, Universidade Estadual Paulista, Universidade Federal do Estado do Rio de Janeiro, Universidade Federal de Santa Catarina, Universidade de São Paulo. Coordinator: Dr. Fernanda Correia Azevedo Dataset organizer: Dr. Rafael da Rocha Fortes""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "BIOGEOMAR | Marine Invertebrates | Mollusca | Gastropoda" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/389e598b-b031-4e03-8290-ad73ee190dca> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:b91df2d66537f2735d1ad1bd1417abb39b8dd8b6>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Happywhale

Dataset credits:
Happywhale and contributors

Abstract:
Happywhale.com is a resource to help you know whales as individuals, and to benefit conservation science with rich data about individual whales.

Supplemental information:
Sightings and images were submitted to Happywhale by contributors. A portion of the Happywhale data were transferred to OBIS-SEAMAP upon the agreement between Happywhale and OBIS-SEAMAP.

There may be duplicate records among Happywhale datasets and other OBIS-SEAMAP datasets. The precision of date/time vary per record. Some records have date accuracy up to year only.

This dataset includes sightings and photos from the following 56 contributors in alphabetic order:

Adam Patton; Ahab; Alain Bidart; Allison Conlon; Alvaro Jaramillo; Amy Markowich; Andy Churn; Bill Tucker; Cees Tineke; Chris Dennis; Conor Ryan; Dale Frink; David L Smith; Deb Hooyman; Elie Vannier; Fabien Chesneau; George Hazelton; Glenda Darling; Hannah Romanowski; Helen Rijkes; Jamie Coleman; Jay Barlow; Jeremy Spencer-Cooper; Jim Wilson; Joel Moore; Joe Olson; Joshua Weil; Kelly M Coursey Gray; Kiana Viceroy; Kimberly Nesbitt; Laurie Kantor; Lesley Matthews; Lisa Menstell; Madelon Rosenplänter; Marian Herz; Marilia Olio; Mark Fiebert; Mike McDonald; Oliver Beagley; Oscar Thomas; Peter Geraerts; Randy Smith; Ricardo Matus; Richard Belbin; Ricky; Robert de PICCIOTTO; Sebastian Mayer; Shayne Lightfoot; Shota Nakagawa; Steve Magness; Suzanne Wafer; Velia Cardin; Victor Laubach; Whale Watcher; Yuan; zhangjinpeng""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,PhotoID;Visual sighting, Observation, Occurrence" ;
