<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:e027211b692c40096037caae758bd9d53e304a8d>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
University of Eldoret, Kenya

Dataset credits:
University of Eldoret, Kenya
Institut Français pour L'Exploitation de la Mer
Kélonia

Abstract:
Satellites tracking of marine turtles was done to identify the main feeding sites of marine turtle nesting in Kenya and assess the interaction between marine turtle and industrial fisheries during their migration. Three nesting sea turtles were tracked from a beach in the North coast of Kenya  (-3.83°S, 39.82°E). One of the turtle moved North to a known foraging creek (Takaungu), while another moved to a foraging ground on the South (Gazi). The third turtle just foraged on the near shore reef within where it was tagged.

Purpose:
The purpose of the tracking was identify the main feeding sites of marine turtle nesting in Kenya and assess the interaction between marine turtle and industrial fisheries during their migration.

Supplemental information:
N/A""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,Marine Animal Survey,Marine Biology,Sea turtles,Animal tagging,Radio transmitters, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "University of Eldoret satellite tracked green sea turtles from Kenya 2012 under SWIOFP" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/aa143416-0e24-4b38-b9f6-27194f1a3ccc> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:e0273792e53ee6deb1b70a1fdc91fb62586bf0a3>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """The following resource is based on content included in a special publication providing an overview of the visiting and nesting birds that have traveled through Nova Scotia’s Bay of Fundy region and made a stopover on Brier Island. This resource contains a list of what is or has been present on and around the island. NOTE: Not all taxa recorded in this list are regular visitors or inhabitants of the island - any migratory species might be found moribund on the coast or taking refuge on a ship’s rigging in the middle of the Bay of Fundy.

This version of the resource contains species presence information only and the observation location information has been generalized to a nominal set of coordinates for the island. Please consult the original publication (Mills and Laviolette, 2011) for detailed information on each species.

The 356 species described in the manuscript were classified based on the American Ornithologists' Union checklist (http://checklist.aou.org/). This resource has retained this classification but each taxon has also been associated with scientific name IDs (life science identifiers/LSIDs) based upon entries in the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) or the Integrated Taxonomic Information System (ITIS).

This version of the resource contains observations from 1880 to January 1, 2016.""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "Birds of Brier Island" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/8e5ebdf7-05f1-4d0d-b9d0-84bf1575926e> ;
.

