<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:f0609fa91bcf4a489302e51642c203c5a3c6aa16>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """Original provider:
Happywhale

Dataset credits:
Happywhale and contributors

Abstract:
Happywhale.com is a resource to help you know whales as individuals, and to benefit conservation science with rich data about individual whales.

Supplemental information:
Sightings and images were submitted to Happywhale by contributors. A portion of the Happywhale data were transferred to OBIS-SEAMAP upon the agreement between Happywhale and OBIS-SEAMAP.

There may be duplicate records among Happywhale datasets and other OBIS-SEAMAP datasets. The precision of date/time vary per record. Some records have date accuracy up to year only.

This dataset includes sightings and photos from the following 9 contributors in alphabetic order:

Anna Astafurova; Barnaby; D Cavagnac; Hans Verdaat; Hondius; Jamie Coleman; Joel Moore; Philip Stone; Pippa Low""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence,PhotoID;Visual sighting, Observation, Occurrence" ;
    ecsummary:name "Happywhale - Bowhead in Arctic Ocean" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/160d5105-5357-4d2b-ba69-e8e3e0d5d626> ;
.

<urn:gleaner.io:oih:obis:data:f064e896a5f8e741d75579d1a9b82c83533cc307>
    a ecsummary:Dataset ;
    ecsummary:description """The study was conducted at the London Bridge breeding colony (38°37′19″S, 142°55′57″E), south-eastern Australia, during the chick-rearing period in 2011/12 and 2012/13. This small mainland colony hosted 68 and 70 active nests in the first and second study periods, respectively. Chick-rearing lasts for 8–10 weeks and consists of two stages: guard stage (2 weeks) where adults make one-day foraging trips while the partner tends to the chicks; and post guard stage (6 to 8 weeks) where adults leave chicks unattended to forage at sea [19]. Breeding adults were instrumented with a GPS data logger (IgotU120, Mobile Action Technology, 44.5×28.5×13 mm), packaged in heat-shrink tubing, programmed to sample location (±10 m) every 2 min. In addition, individuals were deployed with a time-depth recorder (TDR LAT1500, Lotek Wireless Inc, 35×8×8 mm) to measure depth (±0.01 m) every 4 s
Data has been reduced to 5 minute GPS locations if on the surface (depth &lt; 0.5 metre) whilst full dive data has been retained. Complete data is sourced from the cited paper and https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/_At_Sea_Associations_in_Foraging_Little_Penguins_/1137590 on 2021-03-21""" ;
    ecsummary:keywords "Occurrence, Observation" ;
    ecsummary:name "GPS tracking and dive data from 84 foraging trip tracks of Little Penguins (32 female, 31 male) , Port Campbell, Victoria, Australia (2012-2013)" ;
    ecsummary:place "No spatialCoverage" ;
    ecsummary:publisher "obis" ;
    ecsummary:subjectOf <https://obis.org/dataset/7d6d9150-68a4-4aad-8081-29573eadce66> ;
.

